# What every `.ax` file in the tree formats to: sha256(source) sha256(formatted) path.
#
# Materialized from the Rust compiler at commit 1041f11 on 2026-08-08, and
# verified at that commit to be IDENTICAL, all 224 mappings, to what the
# self-hosted compiler built from self_host/main.ax produces. That agreement is
# the provenance of this file, recorded on the last day there were two compilers
# to compare; check-fmt-selfhost.sh has held the self-hosted one to it since.
#
# KEYED BY THE SOURCE HASH, NOT THE PATH, and the path is diagnostic only. `fmt`
# rewrites 221 of these 224 files, so their formatted bytes move whenever anyone
# edits anything, and a golden that churns on every edit gets re-blessed unread.
# Hashing the input means an edited or deleted file RETIRES its entry - the gate
# joins on the source hash and only checks what it still covers - while an
# unedited file is pinned exactly. The floor on surviving entries (150 of 224)
# is what stops retirement from emptying the table into a vacuum.
#
# A hash rather than the bytes because the bytes are 1.4 MB and nobody reviews
# 1.4 MB. What the normal form LOOKS like is reviewed in
# tests/fmt/syntax-zoo.expected.ax and tests/fmt/parity.golden; this file is
# only the claim that it did not move.
#
# Regenerate deliberately, never automatically - a re-bless is how a broken
# formatter becomes the reference:
#
#   axc=$(mktemp -d)/axc
#   .axiom-bin/axiom build --input self_host/main.ax --output "$axc"
#   t=$(mktemp -d)
#   for f in $(find stdlib self_host tests -name '*.ax' | sort); do
#     cp "$f" "$t/c.ax"
#     h=$(shasum -a 256 "$f" | cut -d' ' -f1)
#     if "$axc" fmt "$t/c.ax" >/dev/null 2>&1
#       then printf '%s %s %s\n' "$h" "$(shasum -a 256 "$t/c.ax" | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$f"
#       else printf '%s REFUSED %s\n' "$h" "$f"; fi
#   done
#
# and keep this header. The gate's preservation and rebuild halves do not read
# this file, so they still fail on a formatter re-blessed while wrong.
#
#
# REGENERATED 2026-08-15, deliberately, at 354 entries from 224, after the
# printing work rewrote 445 call sites and retired 83 source hashes at once.
#
# REGENERATED again 2026-08-22, at 451 entries from 354, and this time the
# decay was invisible to every floor in the gate. 308 of the 354 entries still
# matched a file - comfortably above the 250 hit floor - while 143 of the 451
# `.ax` files in the tree had no entry at all, INCLUDING 17 of the 22 compiler
# sources. The table was healthy by every number it reported and had stopped
# covering the code the formatter is most likely to break. The gate now prints
# the unpinned count as well, so that shape is visible before it matters.
#
# Verified before blessing, and this is the check that makes a re-bless safe:
# of the 308 source hashes present in BOTH tables, zero mapped to a different
# formatted hash. So this is a re-keying of files that were edited, not a
# re-blessing of a formatter that moved. `self_host/format.ax` is unchanged in
# the commit that regenerates this.
#
# REGENERATED 2026-08-24, deliberately, at 476 entries from 354, after the
# socket, readiness, signal, entropy, pre-fork and checked-arithmetic work
# added nine `.ax` files to `tests/` and pushed the unpinned count past the
# ceiling of 60. Nothing about the formatter moved: the preservation and
# rebuild halves of check-fmt-selfhost.sh do not read this file and were
# green across the whole regeneration, which is what makes a re-bless here
# a record of new files rather than a formatter being made its own
# REGENERATED 2026-08-25, deliberately, after `AX3042` made an undeclared
# `IO` effect an error: 192 declarations across the compiler, the corpus and
# the standard library gained a `;@axiom:effect(io)` line, which retires a
# source hash for every file touched. Two formatter fixes land with it - an
# AXTAG inside an `(impl ...)` refused the whole file, and an ordinary
# comment inside one was re-emitted after the block it was written in.
#
